E. Proof-facing bridge lemma
  The only selected AP/84m row requiring a rank-6 one-branch rescue core
  is the canonical covering row m=1, duplicated as ap_omit_12_tail_84x01.
  Every AP tail m=2..12 keeps negative naive slack but drops to the
  rank-5 core (5,7,9,11,13) and still has low-rank two-colour
  component escapes.  Thus the bridge target is not a new scalar
  inequality: it is a finite interface between overlap-tax cuts,
  survivor-gate words, and component-cover escape vertices.
  The nonnegative multi_far_84_154 row is a control: it has no one-branch
  deficit and has many two-colour low-rank escapes.

F. Tournament Analysis
  vertices=bridge proof obligations, not runners or raw arcs
  pairwise_observable=predicate retention + one-branch cut exactness + two-colour escape payload + survivor-gate route + scalar-firewall safety
  switch_gauge=higher proof-facing score; ties by declared carrier order
  score_hist={21: 1, 54: 1, 55: 2, 57: 2, 58: 1, 59: 1}
  directed_3cycles=0
  hamiltonian_path_count=1
  hamiltonian_path=canonical_corridor_rank6_bridge -> two_color_component_escape -> one_branch_overlap_rescue_cut -> survivor_gate_word_route -> ap_tail_rank5_descent -> component_conductance_router -> endpoint_spine_wall_handoff -> raw_rescue_rank_scalar

G. Assumption challenge
  Considered vertices: runners, odd blockers, even-half gates, gaps,
  fixed circle sections, section boundaries, wall-crossing events,
  residues, cover arcs, Fourier modes, matroid circuits, survivor gates,
  component-cover graph nodes, endpoint walls, and proof obligations.
  Chosen vertices: bridge obligations between one-branch rescue cuts,
  survivor-gate words, and two-colour component escape certificates.
  Preserves: negative one-branch slack is routed to an exact overlap core
  plus a named two-colour survivor/component escape.
  Destroys: raw runner order, raw rescue-rank scalar meaning, and most
  interval geometry unless endpoint labels and component addresses are
  restored.  Challenged assumption: a rank-6 rescue core signals a broad
  noncanonical obstruction; on this bridge it is the canonical m=1 fence.
